# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000026 (BCF mode)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000026 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000026 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000026 "2026-01-02T16:38:42.555698Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1109/48.757275") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000026 "Natatorial mode that uses a laterally bending midline structure for thrust.

BCF propulsion couples muscle segments and can achieve greater thrust and acceleration than MPF propulsion.

The undulatory BCF locomotion modes also reflect changes in the wavelength and the amplitude envelope of the propulsive wave.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1999"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1109/48.757275") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000026 "BCF propulsion"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1999"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1109/48.757275") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000026 "body and/or caudal fin")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2013"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1089/zeb.2012.0861") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000026 "slapping")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2009"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.021360") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000026 "tail beat")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000026 "BCF mode"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1109/48.757275") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000026 "\"...many fish... switch to BCF modes at higher speeds, and high acceleration rates.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000026 obo:ACTAN_0000019)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000026 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:UBERON_4000164))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000027 (added-mass thrust)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000027 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_quality>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000027 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000027 "2026-01-02T20:25:51.607392Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1109/48.757275") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"As the propulsive wave passes backward along the fish, each small body segment (called propulsion element) generates a force that increases the momentum of the water passing backward. An equal opposing force (the reaction force) is subsequently exerted by the water on the propulsive element.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000027 "Thrust that passes a propulsive wave backward along the body, that increases the momentum of the fluid passing backward, exerting an equal opposing force by the fluid.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1999"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1109/48.757275") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000027 "added-mass method")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2020"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1093/icb/icaa017") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000027 "push")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000027 "added-mass thrust"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1093/icb/icaa017") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000027 "\"Another derived trait of sea snakes (and A. granulatus), the paddle-shaped tail, emphasizes the importance of the entire height of the snake for pushing against water to provide propulsion, whereas nearly all locomotion of snakes on solid surfaces uses only a fraction of the entire surface area to push against the environment.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000027 obo:ACTAN_0000671)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000027 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000086 obo:PATO_0002244))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000028 (exit transition)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000028 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000028 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000028 "2024-09-05T20:35:21.741268Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"When the nest is slightly disturbed, the slaves occasionally come out, and like their masters are much agitated and defend the nest: when the nest is much disturbed and the larvæ and pupæ are exposed, the slaves work energetically with their masters in carrying them away to a place of safety. Hence, it is clear, that the slaves feel quite at home. During the months of June and July, on three successive years, I have watched for many hours several nests in Surrey and Sussex, and never saw a slave either leave or enter a nest...

One evening I visited another community of F. sanguinea, and found a number of these ants entering their nest, carrying the dead bodies of F. fusca (showing that it was not a migration) and numerous pupæ. I traced the returning file burthened with booty, for about forty yards, to a very thick clump of heath, whence I saw the last individual of F. sanguinea emerge, carrying a pupa; but I was not able to find the desolated nest in the thick heath.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000028 "Transition that involves coming out.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2002"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&cbl=18750&diss=y") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000028 "emergence")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2020"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1002/ece3.6372") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000028 "emergency")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1859"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000028 "emerging"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1023/A:1005565304671") Annotation(rdfs:comment "2000"^^xsd:decimal) oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000028 "exit")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000028 "exit transition"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:ACTAN_0000028 obo:ACTAN_0000711)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000028 "\"BEHAVIOUR OF YOUNG A. IBIS... For 12 hours or more before a chick emerges it gives weak, drawn-out notes, rendered \"eeeh\", which after hatching are discomfort notes, given when the chick is cold or roughly handled.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1111/j.1365-3121.1991.tb00141.x") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000028 "\"The genera Lingula and Glottidia possess antero-posteriorly elongated shells, and live within mucus-lined burrows in soft sediment, with the long pedicles anchored at the bottom of their burrows... Both genera burrow by entering the sediment with shell anteriormost and pedicle trailing behind. After burrowing downward for a while, they curve upward and continue burrowing until they emerge from the substrate with the anterior region of the shell.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000028 obo:ACTAN_0000144)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000028 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000086 obo:PATO_0000374))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000029 (entry transition)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000029 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000029 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000029 "2024-09-05T20:37:09.991336Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1177/10597123211040574") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Λ8 is the whole activity, including preparation of going into water and drying of feathers afterwards.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000029 "Ttransition that involves going into.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1859"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000029 "entering"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2000"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1023/A:1005565304671") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000029 "entry")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2005"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1017/S1464793104006700") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000029 "inserting")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000029 "entry transition")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000029 "\"...female Nocomis leptocephalus... entered the pit either by darting directly beneath the male’s tail or by swimming upstream to one side and sidling laterally into the pit.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.3354/esr01312") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000029 "\"VHF-radiotelemetry of C. horridus at Principia College in west–central Illinois revealed post-emergent individuals often retreat into hollow trees, logs, and root holes, particularly during periods of colder temperatures.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000029 obo:ACTAN_0000144)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000029 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000086 obo:PATO_0000375))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000030 (ejection thrust)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000030 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_quality>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000030 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000030 "2026-01-02T21:18:55.959799Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.5962/p.203769") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Fishes in respiring eject water through their gill orifices in a backward direction as pointed out under “Jet propulsion.” It is axiomatic to state that if a jet of any sort whatsoever be ejected at a velocity however low from a body freely suspended, there will be a proportional reaction in the opposite direction.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000030 "Thrust that involves the reaction to an ejected water jet.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000030 "jet propulsion")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000030 "nozzle reaction")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000030 "reactive jet")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000030 "ejection thrust"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1126/science.288.5463.100") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000030 "\"Jet propulsion by bullet-shaped jellyfish is more energy efficient than that of dish-shaped jellyfish, but the vortices produced by dish-shaped animals carry prey to their tentacles more effectively than the vortices produced by bullet-shaped animals.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000030 obo:ACTAN_0000671)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000030 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000086 obo:PATO_0001839))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000031 (amiiform gait)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000031 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000031 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000031 "2026-07-07T09:01:05.49186Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.5962/p.203769") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Amiatus, dorsal undulation. (Amiiform).\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000031 "Ribbon-undulation gait that involves the dorsal fin.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1999"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1109/48.757275") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000031 "amiiform mode")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Amiiform. That form of localized anguilliform undulation...\"") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000031 "amiiform undulation")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000031 "amiiform gait"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000031 "\"Hippocampus hudsonius... when high speed, for a seahorse, is desired it leans forward with the vertebral column nearly horizontal and undulates the dorsal in localized anguilliform fashion.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1109/48.757275") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000031 "\"The best examples of this characteristic are found among the African freshwater electric eels. The anal and caudal fins are missing, while the dorsal fin extends along most of the body length, tapering to a posterior point, and exhibits a large number of fin-rays (up to 200).\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000031 obo:ACTAN_0000016)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000031 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000057 obo:UBERON_0003097))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000032 (gymnotiform gait)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000032 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000032 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000032 "2026-07-07T09:19:11.692969Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.5962/p.203769") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"That form of localized anguilliform undulation found in the anal fin of the Gymnotidae {Gymnotus).\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000032 "Ribbon-undulation gait that involves the anal fin.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1999"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1109/48.757275") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000032 "gymnotiform mode")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Gymnotiform. That form of localized anguilliform undulation...\"") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000032 "gymnotiform undulation")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000032 "movement gymnotiform")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000032 "gymnotiform gait"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000032 obo:ACTAN_0000016)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000032 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000057 obo:UBERON_4000163))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000033 (rajiform gait)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000033 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000033 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000033 "2026-07-07T14:35:11.792867Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1109/48.757275") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Thrust generation involves the passing of vertical undulations along the pectorals that are very large, triangularshaped, and flexible. The amplitude of the undulations increases from the anterior part to the fin apex and then tapers again toward the posterior.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000033 "Ribbon-undulation gait that involves the passing of vertical undulations along the pectorals that are very large, triangular-shaped, and flexible.

The amplitude of the undulations increases from the anterior part to the fin apex and then tapers again toward the posterior.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1999"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1109/48.757275") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000033 "rajiform mode")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000033 "rajiform movement")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Rajiform. That form of localized anguilliform or intermediate
undulation...\"") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000033 "rajiform undulation")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000033 "intermediate undulation")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000033 "rajiform gait"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000033 "\"The families Trygonidae and Myliobatidae have carried the rajiform undulations a step further. The tail has become a mere trailing wisp, which, while it may have some slight course-determining action, appears to trail passively after the animal. Steering is accomplished for the most part by differential action of the pectorals. That is, in curving, the outside “wing” is speeded up or the inside one retarded which swings the animal accordingly.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000033 obo:ACTAN_0000016)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000033 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000057 obo:UBERON_0000151))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000034 (vectoring-undulation gait)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000034 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/gait>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000034 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000034 "2026-07-07T16:49:55.659242Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000034 "Swimming-gait in which convergent rays in a convex-angled fin propogate a wave along the terminal edge of its membrane, by moving the rays serially from side to side.

By varying the way different rays bend within the wave, a fin's longitudinal thrust can be directed.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000034 "caudal undulation")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000034 "vectoring-undulation gait"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.021360") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000034 "\"...the ability of fish to actively control tail shape... and hence modulation and vectoring of
force.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000034 "\"Caudal Undulation:... the rays are convergent instead of parallel.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1109/48.757275") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000034 "\"The fin-rays also perform small longitudinal as well as lateral movements, and they tend to be held like an open fan.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000034 "Two forms may be observed: the first involving a rippling wave, the second a reversible standing wave. Breder says:

\"The central ray of the tail remains still and simply twists on its axis in the latter, while in the former all rays pass through a complete cycle of oscillation. In the method described by Du Bois-Reymond, the upper part of the tail is given a backward thrust diagonally to one side, while the other simultaneously is given a similar one to the opposite side. This has been observed well in Roccus , Epinephelus, and a few others, but appears to be much more rare than the previous method.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000034 obo:ACTAN_0000087)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000034 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:ACTAN_0000023))
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000034 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:ACTAN_0000027))
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000034 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:ACTAN_0000624))
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000034 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000055 obo:ACTAN_0000015))
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000034 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000055 obo:ACTAN_0000069))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000035 (swallow ingestion)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000035 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000035 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000035 "2025-02-09T17:38:05.671847Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "ISBN:9780226315737") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000035 "Ingestion that transports a bolus down the esophagus.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1993"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "ISBN:9780226315737") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000035 "deglutition")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000035 "swallowing"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1993"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "ISBN:9780226315737") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000035 "gulping")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000035 "swallow ingestion"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(skos:closeMatch obo:ACTAN_0000035 "NBO:0000545")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1073/pnas.2122563119") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000035 "\"Penguins have spines only on their dorsal tongue, which allow them to better grip and swallow slippery fish.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000035 obo:ACTAN_0000682)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000035 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000057 obo:UBERON_0001043))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000036 (swarm aggregation)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000036 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/group_behaviour>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000036 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000036 "2025-04-15T15:12:22.036312Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1371/journal.pcbi.1002915") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Swarms are characterized by slow individual motion and a relatively dense, disordered structure.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000036 "Aggregation that is 3-dimensional, characterized by slow individual motion and a relatively dense, disordered structure.

Swarm behavior allows the group to remain in an area.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000036 "swarm"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:date "2023"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://search.proquest.com/openview/28072abf0dcabdc270c0235a6fb16211/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&cbl=18750&diss=y") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Whilst krill can no doubt 'school' at times, as well as 'milling' as explored in flumes in this paper, most literature regards 'swarming' as non-polarized activity. Indeed this paper comments elsewhere on, \"krill transitioning from swarming to schooling\".") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"In nature, they are mostly found as swarms in a polarized manner known as schools.\"") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000036 "school")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000036 "swarm aggregation")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.3354/meps011165") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000036 "\"Although the shape of copepod swarms depended on the species and the localities, the most common type was irregular ball-shaped. Each ball-shaped swarm was most often spherical or ovoid, but sometimes became stretched out longitudinally. These swarms were fairly distinct with clear boundaries. Their diameters ranged from a few centimeters to over l m, but most swarms were smaller than 50 cm in diameter. They usually stayed at the same position and contacted the bottom or were a little above it. Copepods within a swarm were hopping up and sinking down against the slight water current with their heads diagonally downward.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000036 "\"By swarming of the young we mean swimming in very dense concentrations near some object.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000036 obo:ACTAN_0000411)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000036 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000055 obo:ACTAN_0000048))
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000036 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000055 obo:ACTAN_0000662))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000037 (CFP gait)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000037 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000037 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000037 "2026-07-07T20:03:40.754143Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.5962/p.203769") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"While the caudal is operated primarily by the action of the body muscles, many fishes at times may be seen to undulate the tail fin vertically and move forward slowly without any other apparent effort.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000037 "Vectoring-undulation gait that involves the tail fin.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2008"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.020263") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000037 "CFP")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2008"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.020263") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000037 "caudal fin-wave propagation")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000037 "caudal undulation")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000037 "CFP gait"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.020263") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000037 "\"The propagation of a CFP is observable in a parrot cichlid that swims in still water or against a current. When a parrot cichlid initiates a forward sinking, the pectoral fins first induce a headdown pitching moment that tips the head down. Afterwards, the parrot cichlid adopts a stable tilted-down swimming posture. In this posture, the transverse amplitude (i.e. lateral displacement) of the dorsally propagating fin wave gradually increases from the ventral to the dorsal edges of the caudal fin.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000037 obo:ACTAN_0000034)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000037 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000057 obo:UBERON_4000164))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000038 (diodontiform gait)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000038 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000038 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000038 "2026-07-07T20:05:06.352322Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.5962/p.203769") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Other species, as Spheroides, undulate their pectorals in a manner similar to the way in which the tail is sometimes employed, as described under “ Caudal undulation.”\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000038 "Vectoring-undulation gait that involves a pectoral fin.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000038 "diodontiform movement")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000038 "diodontiform gait"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000038 obo:ACTAN_0000034)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000038 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000057 obo:UBERON_0000151))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000039 (seahorse gait)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000039 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000039 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000039 "2026-07-07T20:05:43.911989Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.5962/p.203769") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"...“Caudal undulation'', not of great importance to any fish. This, at times, is similar to the dorsal movements of Hippocampus, and with it completes the list of every known fundamental motion.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000039 "Vectoring-undulation gait that involves the back nearly vertical, and the dorsal fin posteriorly positioned.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000039 "seahorse gait"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000039 "\"Hippocampus hudsonius which usually moves with the backbone in a nearly vertical position is necessarily modified accordingly. In rising or falling in the water appropriate localized undulations in the dorsal give the desired motion on account of its position.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000039 obo:ACTAN_0000034)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000039 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000057 obo:UBERON_0003097))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000040 (pectoral-fin abducting)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000040 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000040 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000040 "2026-07-12T07:22:23.775029Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1242/jeb.204.8.1459") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Pectoral fin-beat cycles started with the fins pressed against the body (maximally adducted), moving away (abducting) from the body to some maximum point of excursion and returning (adducting) to the original position.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000040 "Abducting in which a pectoral fin is moving away from the body.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"...extending a single pectoral...\"") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000040 "pectoral extending"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2001"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.204.8.1459") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Pectoral fin-beat cycles started with the fins pressed against the body (maximally adducted), moving away (abducting) from the body to some maximum point of excursion...\"") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000040 "pectoral-fin abducting"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000040 "\"In Tilapia natalensis the pectoral fins consist of 13 soft furcated fin rays... Contraction of the m. abductores as a whole operates the fin erect \"in line\"; the fin advances as a flat plane.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000040 obo:ACTAN_0000738)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000040 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000057 obo:UBERON_0000151))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000041 (sideways)

AnnotationAssertion(dc:source obo:ACTAN_0000041 "doi:10.1242/jeb.120.1.283")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000041 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000041 "2024-09-06T20:33:45.814629Z")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:ACTAN_0000041 "This class is a temporary placeholder until it a new term request is accepted by an external ontology. This term will then be obsoleted in favour of that external new term.")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000041 "sideways"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000041 "\"(decapod crustaceans) typically use octapedal locomotion and walk sideways\"")
