# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000077 (state pattern)

AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000077 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000077 "2024-09-10T09:52:40.187376Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1859"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303") oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000077 "living state")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.27.3.264") oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000077 "phase")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1534/genetics.120.303539") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"In each locomotion state, C. elegans animals express a characteristic set of locomotor parameters over a long-lasting, stable time period. C. elegans locomotion is comprised of just a few basic building blocks...\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000077 "Component pattern in which animals express a characteristic set of basic building blocks over a long-lasting, stable time period.

Switches to new states are regulated by integration of the transitioning or prolonging influences of internal and external cues, as well as historical associations.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2016"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1111/1365-2656.12379") Annotation(rdfs:comment "\"...2 week bouts... 
...bouts of long distance movement...\"") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Given the relative nature of modules, sequences and states, bout is at times used correctly as a synonym for any of them.") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000077 "bout")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000077 "state"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000077 "attitude")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000077 "state pattern")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1534/genetics.120.30353") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000077 "\"Among the most familiar of these behavioral states are easily observable arousal states like sleep and wakefulness, as well as feeding states controlled by hunger and satiety. Animals also exhibit emotional states, like states of heightened anxiety or depression, as well as cognitive states, for example, in the contexts of attention or working memory.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000077 "\"In very cold water when Chaenobryttus is in a state of semi-hibernation they may sometimes be observed with no fin movement at all and with both sets of paired fins securely pressed against the sides. At such times the respiration is extremely superficial and appears to have little reactive effect. However, after a long period of time it was observed that they do move forward ever so slightly and that very occasionally the pectorals are brought into play to regain the previous position. However, they generally allow themselves to come in contact with some piece of brush or other object in which case the friction is sufficient to stop motion.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "https://www.jstor.org/stable/4533591") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000077 "\"Behaviors may be regarded either as events or as states. Events are instantaneous; states have appreciable durations. Of course, in reality, the performance of any kind of behavior takes some amount of time, however brief. But if we consider behaviors at the moment of their onset, or at any other single defining instant, then we are recording events. We can, for example, record that an animal assumes a sitting posture, an act that occurs at an instant (and is therefore an event), or that the animal is seated (a state).\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000077 obo:ACTAN_0000011)

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000078 (activity behaviour)

AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000078 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000078 "2024-09-10T15:53:05.898277Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1111/j.1600-0706.2011.19044.x") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Since animals change their movement patterns through time as a response to changing environmental factors, prey abundance, habitats etc., it is necessary to regard the system as a hierarchy of two subprocesses: An underlying behavior process that controls the switching between a number of diferent movement states; and a derived process that describes the movement dynamics conditional on the behavioral state.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000078 "Behaviour that describes the movement dynamics conditional on a behavioural state.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000078 "act")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000078 "action"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2002"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&cbl=18750&diss=y") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000078 "active behavior")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000078 "activity")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000078 "activity behaviour")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000078 "\"...the bright red system in Hemichromis and the black in Tilapia natalensis are only seen when the fish also shows definite courtship activities...\"")
AnnotationAssertion(skos:narrowMatch obo:ACTAN_0000078 "NBO:0000338")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000078 obo:ACTAN_0000002)

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000079 (chain pattern)

AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000079 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000079 "2024-09-10T19:12:57.574625Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1089/zeb.2011.0690") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"This behavior sequence matrix was analyzed using a first-order Markov chain analysis to identify nonrandom transitions between behavioral elements (i.e., nonrandom temporal associations between behavioral patterns).\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000079 "Component pattern that involves sequential, nonrandom, transitions between behavioral elements.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1965"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Given the relative nature of modules, sequences and states, bout is at times used correctly as a synonym for any of them.") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000079 "bout")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1966"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.2307/4083155") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000079 "chain"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1965"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000079 "compound")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1961"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000079 "sequence")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000079 "chain pattern")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000079 "\"Head-shakes are very common and may occur in a variety of situations. They are characteristic of bouts of nibbling preening and are closely associated with bill-dipping. The usual sequence is: bill-dip + head-shake + preening movement.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1146/annurev-neuro-071714-033857") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000079 "\"One example is dyadic fighting in male zebrafish, which can be divided into assessment and postresolution phases. Each phase is structured clearly in different behaviors. In the assessment phase, fish exhibit display, circle, and bite behaviors. In the resolution phase, agonistic behaviors (bite, chase, strike) are always initiated by the winner.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000079 "\"Spawning behaviors... in the nest-building minnows, the bluehead chub, Nocomis leptocephalus, and river chub, N. micropogon... were classified into six sequential categories (interim, approach, alignment, run, clasp, dissociation)...\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000079 obo:ACTAN_0000011)

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000080 (module pattern)

AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000080 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000080 "2024-09-10T19:19:45.70162Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000080 "Component pattern that involves a simple action, comprised of at least one body part movement, and produced from within a constrained set of behavioural possibilities.

The duration may be more or less extended.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2025"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1007/s10988-025-09438-8") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "atomic element")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2019"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022") Annotation(rdfs:comment "Given the relative nature of modules, sequences and states, bout is at times used correctly as a synonym for any of them.") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "bout")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2020"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1534/genetics.120.303539") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "building block")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "element")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2019"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "module")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2019"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "motor primitive")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2019"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "moveme")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1961"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "unit")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "component")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1974"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://www.jstor.org/stable/4533591") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "event")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2019"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000080 "synergy")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000080 "module pattern")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000080 "\"Hierarchical clustering separated bouts into seven types, corresponding to J-turns, approach swims, ‘‘slow 1’’ swims, capture strikes, ‘‘slow 2’’ swims, high-angle turns (HATs), and routine turns... J-turns, approach swims, and capture strikes occupy the prey capture region of the behavioral space... ‘‘Slow 2’’ swims, HATs, and routine turns were performed by fish during spontaneous swimming. ‘‘Slow 1’’ swims contributed to both prey capture and spontaneous behavior. Thus, taking transition dynamics into account, we could relate regions of our behavioral space to known bout types of zebrafish larvae and demonstrate the differential contributions of these swims to spontaneous and prey capture behavior.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "https://www.jstor.org/stable/4533591") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000080 "\"Behaviors may be regarded either as events or as states. Events are instantaneous; states have appreciable durations. Of course, in reality, the performance of any kind of behavior takes some amount of time, however brief. But if we consider behaviors at the moment of their onset, or at any other single defining instant, then we are recording events. We can, for example, record that an animal assumes a sitting posture, an act that occurs at an instant (and is therefore an event), or that the animal is seated (a state).\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000080 "\"It is believed that complex, flexible behavior arises as a result of animals chaining together simpler, more stereotyped movements. These movements have been termed motor primitives, synergies, movemes, or behavioral modules. However, whether such modules truly constitute stereotyped, invariant movements or whether they merely reflect extremes in a behavioral continuum remains unclear.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000080 "Behaviour modules may be stereotyped or lie on a continuum, partly depending on whether they rely on internal or external stimuli. An observor of a particular instance may of course label it as a 'module' and add the characteristic 'flexible' or 'inflexible' should they wish, but for now this class will not be subdivided, nor one interpretation enforced, since knowing the origin of a stimulus is so difficult.")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000080 obo:ACTAN_0000011)

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000081 (placement kinematics)

AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000081 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000081 "2024-09-13T20:38:41.979738Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.198.1.79") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000081 "Kinematics that positions a body part in a relatively fixed orientation.

An object may still move in relation to its environment (e.g. sliding and viscoelastic flexing).")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1995"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.198.1.79") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000081 "placement"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.27.3.264") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000081 "condition")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"upright position\"") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000081 "position")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1242/jeb.27.3.264") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000081 "state")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000081 "placement kinematics"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1537/ase.107.141") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000081 "\"Mother prevents access to breast by placing her arm across her nipple... She may also lie with breasts pressed to the ground or branch. Part of mother's weaning of her infant.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.3389/fetho.2022.1066186") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000081 "\"relatively ornate changes in feather and wing placement\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000081 obo:ACTAN_0000747)

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000082 (movement kinematics)

AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000082 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000082 "2024-09-13T20:47:24.16678Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1093/icb/icac125") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000082 "Kinematics which displaces or reorients part of the body.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2006"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1093/auk/123.4.967") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000082 "action pattern")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1926"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/p.203769") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000082 "motion")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1922"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.2307/2939673") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000082 "movement")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1859"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000082 "moving"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000082 "movement kinematics")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000082 "\"Larvae capture prey from below with stereotyped jaw and tail movements\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1093/auk/123.4.967") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000082 "\"...Action Pattern... describes body, appendage, and ornament motions.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000082 obo:ACTAN_0000747)

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000083 (splaying movement)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000083 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/movement>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000083 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000083 "2025-06-11T11:16:02.75584Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1961"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf") oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000083 "opening")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000083 "spreading")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1537/ase.107.141") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000083 "Movement that spreads similar parts from each other more distally than proximally.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2022"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1093/icb/icac061") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000083 "splaying"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1977"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://digit.snm.ku.dk/Documentsonline/GalatheaReports/galathea-vol.14-pp_049-108.pdf") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000083 "arching")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1957"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&context=wilson_bulletin") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000083 "fanning")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000083 "splaying movement")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:ACTAN_0000083 obo:ACTAN_0000624)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:ACTAN_0000083 obo:ACTAN_0000726)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:ACTAN_0000083 obo:ACTAN_0000740)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "https://digit.snm.ku.dk/Documentsonline/GalatheaReports/galathea-vol.14-pp_049-108.pdf") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000083 "\"Also, the muscles supplying the pectoral fin rays are better suited for an arching outward, upward, forward movement than for a downward type of movement necessary for probing and feeding in a bottom fish standing on stilts. Moreover, in some species of Bathypterois the pectoral fin rays are insufliciently elongate to reach the substrate while the fish is standing upon it.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1007/s00265-014-1841-5") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000083 "\"The semelparous giant Australian cuttlefish, Sepia apama... male typically initiates the mating by spreading his arms and grasping the female’s head.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.2307/4083155") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000083 "\"...some species spread their tails symmetrically...\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000083 obo:ACTAN_0000082)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000083 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000086 obo:PATO_0002452))

