# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000094 (sound kinetics)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000094 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/kinetics>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000094 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000094 "2026-03-18T11:20:37.697715Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&cbl=18750&diss=y") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000094 "Kinetics that produces an audible, sonic wavelength.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1967"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000094 "noise")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1859"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000094 "sound production")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000094 "sound kinetics"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1093/icb/41.2.137") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000094 "\"Humans and other species often stop and listen if they need to detect a faint sound or to make a fine auditory discrimination. Orthopteran insects offer an extreme example. Because the auditory receptors of crickets are located on their forelegs, walking produces excitation of auditory receptors in the absence of sound and suppression of action potentials in response to sounds (Schildberger et al., 1988). Female crickets orienting to a male calling song pause frequently and change direction primarily during pauses.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000094 "\"How a nerve comes to be sensitive to light, hardly concerns us more than how life itself first originated; but I may remark that several facts make me suspect that any sensitive nerve may be rendered sensitive to light, and likewise to those coarser vibrations of the air which produce sound.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1007/BF01609520") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000094 "\"The species we observe, mainly the successful survivors of different selection pressures, may be using structures to serve more than one function. The case of pharyngeal teeth which function both in sound production, and in feeding is a relevant example.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000094 obo:ACTAN_0000147)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000094 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000086 obo:PATO_0001519))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000095 (reliability influence)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000095 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000095 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000095 "2026-03-27T10:38:02.585157Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000095 "Influence that signals quality.

There may be a verifiability cost in testing honesty or punishing dishonesty.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1967"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000095 "advertising")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2012"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000095 "costly signal")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2012"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000095 "handicap")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2012"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000095 "honesty")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2012"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000095 "index signal")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000095 "reliability influence"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000095 "\"...anal-fin fanning also may serve as a courtship display to attract females. In N. leptocephalus, the anterior anal-fin rays become bright white in breeding males compared to pale yellow or cream colored in nonbreeding males. White anal-fin rays and anal-fin fanning may exaggerate the appearance of a male releasing milt and perhaps stimulate in females a sensory bias for such males. Anal-fin fanning also may facilitate the release of chemical cues (e.g., milt, pheromones) into the spawning pit.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1098/rspb.2014.0876") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000095 "\"...costly signalling theory provides the ultimate, adaptive rationale for honest signalling, whereas the index hypothesis describes one proximate (and potentially very general) mechanism for achieving honesty.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000095 "\"The problem of reliability in signalling theory consists in explaining why signallers typically do not deceive receivers, given the incentive to do so. One solution to the problem appeals to the costs of signalling to explain reliability...

The key insight underlying costly signalling theory is that signal reliability can be maintained, despite conflicting sender and receiver interests, if dishonesty, while possible, is not profitable. The insight can apply, even if honest signals are free, because costly punishment of dishonesty counts as a kind of imposed signal cost.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000095 obo:ACTAN_0000438)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000095 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:ACTAN_0000114))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000096 (rearrangement pattern)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000096 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_quality>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000096 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000096 "2026-04-14T09:11:06.431784Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Different authors had suggested that the phenomenon of live and of evolution of living systems is related to the concept of increase in the order of the system; where order is considered to be negative entropy.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000096 "Routine pattern in which the order of a system is changed.

Order is considered to be negative entropy.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1965"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000096 "rearranging")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1957"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&context=wilson_bulletin") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000096 "disarrangement")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1984"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/0022-5193(84)90009-2") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000096 "negentropy")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000096 "rearrangement pattern"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000096 "\"The primary function of the body-shake seems to be the removal of water drops from the bird's feathers, which is achieved by the centrifugal effect of the movements. It may also help to rearrange the feathers when they have been disordered (e.g. by preeening).\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000096 obo:ACTAN_0000397)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000096 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000086 obo:PATO_0002436))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000097 (exudation activity)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000097 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000097 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000097 "2024-10-21T08:32:55.156149Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&context=wilson_bulletin") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Instead of spraying acid secretions when disturbed, these species exude from the anal glands fluid droplets having the scent of rancid butter.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000097 "Activity that involves a fluid glandular secretion.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1957"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&context=wilson_bulletin") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000097 "exudation")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2002"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&cbl=18750&diss=y") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000097 "secretion"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000097 "exudation activity"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(skos:closeMatch obo:ACTAN_0000097 "NBO:0000041")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1093/beheco/12.5.534") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000097 "\"A second example concerns thermoregulation in Apis dorsata and A. florea... at higher ambient temperatures, nest cooling is enhanced by evaporation of water: some workers exude water droplets, while other bees fan their wings.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&context=wilson_bulletin") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000097 "\"DOLICHODERINAE (Sting vestigial; exude repugnatorial liquid from anal glands): Iridomyrmex detectus (F. Smith)\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.cub.2021.09.082") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000097 "\"Bodily secretions include saliva, wax, silk and even excrement, all of which can serve as effective glue for cementing external materials together.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000097 obo:ACTAN_0000078)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000097 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000057 obo:UBERON_0000456))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000098 (exudation anointment)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000098 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000098 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000098 "2026-04-19T08:16:10.743839Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&cbl=18750&diss=y") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Tail rubbing, a behavior found only in large-bodied rattlesnakes, was mostly performed by gravid females. This behavior is associated with cloacal sac discharge and its function appears to be to spread exudates over the body of the snake.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000098 "Anointment that spreads an exudate over the body.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2002"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&cbl=18750&diss=y") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000098 "spread exudate")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000098 "exudation anointment"@en)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000098 obo:ACTAN_0000788)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000098 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000057 obo:UBERON_0000456))

