# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000828 (dyad membership)

AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000828 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000828 "2025-09-09T13:56:01.280512Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1016/j.yhbeh.2021.105062") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"For example, pair bonding is often conflated with “pair living”, or even the simple observation of a physical association between two animals.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000828 "Membership that involves an association between two animals.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2011"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1089/zeb.2011.0690") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000828 "dyad"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000828 "dyad membership")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1089/zeb.2011.0690") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000828 "\"...in dyadic fights male zebrafish express highly structured behavior and the outcome of these fights has an impact on their subsequent behavior.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.yhbeh.2021.105062") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000828 "\"...many avian researchers apply the term to same-sex dyads...\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000828 obo:ACTAN_0000108)

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000829 (pairing participation)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000829 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_realization>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000829 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000829 "2025-09-09T13:56:15.464199Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:comment "Bear in mind that the definition is essentially that of pair-bonding minus its affective components.") Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1016/j.yhbeh.2021.105062") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"Further, a pair bond must endure for a biologically relevant duration of time: we propose that in order to be considered a pair bond, the relationship between two individuals must last longer than one reproductive cycle [e.g., prairie vole, sleepy lizards]. In addition to duration, we posit that pair bonds must have some degree of strength or quality that is measurable, that both individuals must be sexually mature, and that the bond must be specific to a particular individual. Specificity refers to the display of certain behaviors restricted to the pair-bonded partner, including but not limited to territoriality/mate guarding [e.g., cichlids, titi monkeys, albatrosses], separation distress [e.g. titi monkey], partner preference [e.g., titi monkeys, prairie voles], and stress buffering [e.g., titi monkey], which are displayed in association with the chosen partner rather than indiscriminately among conspecifics...

However, the pair must display behavior that is, at minimum, selective and exclusive to a partner and maintained in some way across time and reflective of an affective component to the relationship. It is this last component that is often missing from non-mammalian definitions of pair bonding;\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000829 "Participation which is selective and exclusive to mature partners and maintained across time.

The relationship MUST be between two sexually mature individuals, last longer than one reproductive cycle, and display strong, partner-restricted behaviours such as territoriality or mate guarding.

They MAY participate in sexual behavior with each other, co-parent, and participate in a coordinated activity together.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2021"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.yhbeh.2021.105062") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000829 "pair living")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000829 "pairing"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000829 "bonding")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1965"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000829 "pair bonding")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000829 "pairing participation")
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:ACTAN_0000829 obo:ACTAN_0000465)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:ACTAN_0000829 obo:ACTAN_0000471)
AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:seeAlso obo:ACTAN_0000829 obo:ACTAN_0000667)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "https://www.science.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/sites/11/2024/05/s22rbz145-157.pdf") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000829 "\"Whipcoral gobies in the genus Bryaninops are habitat-specialists that live in male-female pairs...\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.yhbeh.2021.105062") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000829 "\"Wood-feeding cockroaches (Salganea taiwanensis) mate and live as pairs; quite notably, after choosing a mate, paired cockroaches chew off each other’s wing. After cannibalizing their pair mate’s non-fleshy wings, paired individuals mate and produce multiple cycles of offspring that are cared for by both parents. Paired woodfeeding cockroaches mate with each other exclusively and for life.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000829 obo:ACTAN_0000409)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000829 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000055 obo:ACTAN_0000828))
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000829 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000086 obo:PATO_0000498))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000830 (bonding pairing)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000830 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_mental_process>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000830 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000830 "2025-09-09T13:56:38.49968Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1016/j.yhbeh.2021.105062") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"However, the pair must display behavior that is, at minimum, selective and exclusive to a partner and maintained in some way across time and reflective of an affective component to the relationship. It is this last component that is often missing from non-mammalian definitions of pair bonding; however, we would argue that a relationship cannot be called a pair bond without some level of engagement of affective systems, whether those be related to motivation, stress, reward, or all of the above. Otherwise, it is difficult to imagine to what the word “bond” is actually referring.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000830 "Pairing that is reflective of an affective component.

This may relate to motivation, stress, or reward, with measurable separation distress, partner preference, and specific stress buffering.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1965"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000830 "pair bonding"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000830 "bonding pairing")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.yhbeh.2021.105062") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000830 "\"Zebra finches have been studied in a wide range of paradigms that have both observationally and experimentally determined that they demonstrate strongly coordinated pair behavior and mate-guarding, preference for the familiar partner, and distress upon separation from the partner.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000830 obo:ACTAN_0000829)
SubClassOf(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.yhbeh.2021.105062") obo:ACTAN_0000830 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:ACTAN_0000447))
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000830 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:MFOEM_000195))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000831 (formation pairing)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000831 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_realization>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000831 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000831 "2025-09-10T10:17:52.648537Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(rdfs:isDefinedBy "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"; we think it better to consider as pair formation all occurrences from the first meeting of the future partners until a definite sexual bond has been established that can only be broken by the appearance of abnormal conditions.\"") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000831 "Pairing that includes all occurrences from the first meeting of the future partners until a definite sexual bond has been established that can only be broken by the appearance of abnormal conditions.

In some genera, this is the prelude to eventual pair bonding.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2011"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1590/S1679-62252011005000018") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000831 "couple formation")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000831 "pair formation"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000831 "mate changing")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000831 "formation pairing")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.2307/1367395") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000831 "\"For example, Willis wrote of early pair formation in Bicolored Antbirds, “She seemed to push him backward in her cringing advance . . . ,” and continued that the male, having been forced off his perch, then went and brought the female some food.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1590/S1679-62252011005000018") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000831 "\"Laetacara araguaiae is a small cichlid species...

The establishment of territory by males preceded pair formation. Adult males established territories and defended them with attacks and threats against intruder fish, especially of conspecific ones. Each male was observed defending only one territory. Pairs were assumed to be formed when female also engaged in territorial defense. After that, male and female alternated among courtship behaviors, nest digging and territorial defense.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.2307/4083155") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000831 "\"In birds with a long period between pair formation and copulation, displays preceding copulation need not be as specific as those in birds in which copulation occurs at the initial meeting.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000831 "\"Preen-behind-wing occurs in many species during the period of pair-formation...\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000831 obo:ACTAN_0000829)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000831 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000055 obo:ACTAN_0000898))
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000831 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:RO_0000086 obo:PATO_0000083))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000832 (affiliation pairing)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000832 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000832 "Ditch Townsend")
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:creation_date obo:ACTAN_0000832 "2025-09-10T16:01:46.190802Z")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") oboInOwl:hasDefinition obo:ACTAN_0000832 "Pairing that involves mutual signaling.")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "2021"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.yhbeh.2021.105062") Annotation(skos:scopeNote "\"...higher rates of affiliation relative to aggression signaling...\"") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000832 "affiliation signaling")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1967"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000832 "pair-displaying"@en)
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1950"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853981X00220") oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000832 "greeting ceremony")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:date "1965"^^xsd:decimal) Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") Annotation(rdfs:seeAlso obo:ACTAN_0000837) oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym obo:ACTAN_0000832 "nest relief")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:contributor "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151") rdfs:label obo:ACTAN_0000832 "affiliation pairing")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000832 "\"A female Chiloe Wigeon, alone on the water, gave a loud descrescendo call; immediately a male appeared from behind some reeds and swam toward the female. On meeting, the female performed a Preen and a few seconds later it was given by the male. The two swam away together.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000832 "\"One of the displays of the Andean Goose involves lifting the wings to form an arch over the back. While calling loudly and lifting the wings in this way, both male and female repeatedly give rapid bursts of Tail-wagging.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1016/j.yhbeh.2021.105062") skos:example obo:ACTAN_0000832 "\"Pair-bonded fish exhibit several affiliative behaviors. In cichlids, pair-bonded individuals display parallel swimming, tail beating, circling, greeting, and affiliative bites.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(skos:narrowMatch obo:ACTAN_0000832 "NBO:0020071")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000832 "\"...the stereotyped movements preceding \"nest relief\" of one member of a breeding pair by another in birds... function to regulate or coordinate cooperation between the sexes.\"")
AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(dc:source "doi:10.1163/156853965x00138") skos:scopeNote obo:ACTAN_0000832 "\"The members of a pair may give Preen-behind-wing in several situations. It often occurs when the birds meet after a separation... Another situation is after a hostile encounter... At other times, the display is given by birds which are resting or preening normally side by side... Preen-behind-wing occurs in many species... while the bond is becoming stronger.\"")
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000832 obo:ACTAN_0000829)
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000832 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:ACTAN_0000414))
SubClassOf(obo:ACTAN_0000832 ObjectSomeValuesFrom(obo:BFO_0000051 obo:ACTAN_0000465))

# Class: obo:ACTAN_0000833 (hindlimb extension)

AnnotationAssertion(terms:conformsTo obo:ACTAN_0000833 <https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity>)
AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:created_by obo:ACTAN_0000833 "Ditch Townsend")
